import type { QuestionSeed } from "./types";

/** Hard Questions about doctrine and church life. Original FCGBS text; where Christians differ, the views are stated fairly. */
export const CHURCH_QUESTIONS: QuestionSeed[] = [
  { slug: "faith-and-works-paul-and-james", category: "Salvation and the Christian life",
    title: "Faith and works: do Paul and James contradict each other?",
    summary: "They do not. Paul argues that no one is made right with God by keeping the law; James argues that a faith which produces no obedience is dead. They use 'justified' in different senses (declared righteous before God versus shown to be genuine) and fight different errors (legalism versus empty profession). Together they teach that we are saved by grace through faith alone, but not by a faith that is alone.",
    scriptures: ["Romans 3:21-28", "Romans 4:1-8", "Galatians 2:15-21", "Ephesians 2:8-10", "James 2:14-26", "Genesis 15:6", "Genesis 22:1-12", "Titus 3:4-8"],
    readings: ["Douglas Moo, The Letter of James (Pillar)", "Thomas Schreiner, Romans (BECNT)", "J. I. Packer, Knowing God (on justification)", "N. T. Wright, Paul: A Biography"],
    exegetical: `## Paul's claim
"Therefore we conclude that a man is justified by faith without the deeds of the law" (Romans 3:28, KJV). He uses Abraham: "Abraham believed God, and it was counted unto him for righteousness" (Romans 4:3, KJV, quoting Genesis 15:6). In Ephesians 2:8–10 salvation is "by grace... through faith" and "not of works", yet we are "created in Christ Jesus unto good works".

## James's claim
"Ye see then how that by works a man is justified, and not by faith only" (James 2:24, KJV). He uses Abraham too, offering Isaac (James 2:21; Genesis 22). "Faith without works is dead" (James 2:26, KJV).

## Resolving the tension
1. **Different "works".** Paul means works of the law as a way to earn favour (circumcision, ceremonial rules) as a basis for acceptance with God. James means the works of love and obedience that flow from faith.
2. **Different "justified".** Paul uses *dikaioō* (G1344) for being declared righteous by God. James uses it for being shown to be righteous, vindicated before others by deeds (as in Matthew 11:19 "wisdom is justified of her children"). Genesis 15:6 happened decades before Genesis 22, so James 2:23 reads the later obedience as having fulfilled ("the scripture was fulfilled") the earlier declaration.
3. **Different opponents.** Paul opposes those who add law-keeping to faith as a requirement (Galatians). James opposes those who claim faith while ignoring the needy (James 2:15–16).
4. **Different "faith".** For James, the faith of demons (James 2:19) is intellectual agreement. Paul speaks of "faith which worketh by love" (Galatians 5:6, KJV).

## Both agree
Paul insists on obedience: "faith which worketh by love" (Galatians 5:6) and the fruit of the Spirit. James insists on grace: "Of his own will begat he us with the word of truth" (James 1:18, KJV).`,
    archaeological: `The background is first-century Judaism: some taught that covenant membership was marked by law-keeping ("works of the law"). The Dead Sea Scroll 4QMMT ("some works of the Torah") uses the same phrase as Paul and shows what it meant: specific observances that marked the boundary of the covenant community. This supports reading Paul's "works of the law" as boundary-marking and law-keeping as a way to belong, not merely good deeds. James writes to Jewish Christians scattered abroad, whose experience included trials and the temptation to a faith of words.`,
    apostolic: `Peter agrees with both: "Wherefore the rather, brethren, give diligence to make your calling and election sure" (2 Peter 1:10, KJV); and he says believers are saved by the grace of the Lord Jesus (Acts 15:11). John writes that "he that saith, I know him, and keepeth not his commandments, is a liar" (1 John 2:4, KJV). The apostolic message: faith in Christ saves, and real faith bears fruit.`,
    prophetic: `The prophets already spoke of the heart-change that produces obedience: "I will put my law in their inward parts, and write it in their hearts" (Jeremiah 31:33, KJV); "I will put my spirit within you, and cause you to walk in my statutes" (Ezekiel 36:27, KJV). Under the new covenant the Spirit enables the obedience that the law demanded.`,
    theological: `## Summary for teaching
- **The ground of justification** is Christ's finished work, received by faith alone.
- **The evidence of justification** is a changed life.
- Works are not the root but the fruit.
- Reformation slogan: "We are justified by faith alone, but the faith that justifies is never alone" (a saying attributed to Calvin and others).

## Pastoral errors to avoid
- **Legalism:** adding conditions to grace.
- **Antinomianism (cheap grace):** claiming grace while ignoring holiness (Romans 6:1–2).`,
    views: `Catholic teaching sees justification as including the believer's inner renewal and cooperation with grace; Protestants emphasise the declaration of righteousness based on Christ's merit. The "New Perspective on Paul" (Sanders, Dunn, Wright) stresses the covenant context of "works of the law". Evangelicals broadly agree on the harmony of Paul and James.`,
    pastoral: `To the anxious: your assurance rests on Christ, not on your performance. To the careless: if you claim faith but live as you please, examine yourself (2 Corinthians 13:5). Show both grace and expectation.` },

  { slug: "can-a-believer-lose-salvation", category: "Salvation and the Christian life",
    title: "Can a believer lose their salvation?",
    summary: "Christians hold two serious positions. One (eternal security or perseverance of the saints) says that those whom God saves He keeps, and that those who finally fall away were never truly His. The other (conditional security) says that believers can abandon Christ through persistent unbelief. Both warn against complacency and both give real assurance to those who trust Christ.",
    scriptures: ["John 6:37-40", "John 10:27-30", "Romans 8:31-39", "Philippians 1:6", "Ephesians 1:13-14", "Hebrews 6:4-8", "Hebrews 10:26-31", "Colossians 1:21-23", "1 John 2:19", "2 Peter 2:20-22", "Jude 1:24"],
    readings: ["Thomas Schreiner and Ardel Caneday, The Race Set Before Us", "I. Howard Marshall, Kept by the Power of God", "Four Views on Eternal Security (Zondervan)", "D. A. Carson, Divine Sovereignty and Human Responsibility"],
    exegetical: `## Texts that stress security
- "All that the Father giveth me shall come to me; and him that cometh to me I will in no wise cast out" (John 6:37, KJV).
- "My sheep hear my voice, and I know them, and they follow me: And I give unto them eternal life; and they shall never perish, neither shall any man pluck them out of my hand" (John 10:27–28, KJV).
- "Being confident of this very thing, that he which hath begun a good work in you will perform it until the day of Jesus Christ" (Philippians 1:6, KJV).
- Romans 8:38–39: nothing "shall be able to separate us from the love of God".
- Jude 24: "Now unto him that is able to keep you from falling".

## Texts that warn
- Hebrews 6:4–6 speaks of those who were enlightened, tasted the heavenly gift and "fall away".
- Hebrews 10:26: "For if we sin wilfully after that we have received the knowledge of the truth, there remaineth no more sacrifice for sins" (KJV).
- Colossians 1:23: "If ye continue in the faith grounded and settled".
- 2 Peter 2:20–22: those who escaped the world's pollutions and are again entangled.
- John 15:1–6: branches in the vine that do not bear fruit are cut off.

## How the warning passages are read
1. **Warnings are the means of perseverance** (Reformed view): God preserves His people by using warnings; those who finally fall were never genuinely united with Christ. "They went out from us, but they were not of us: for if they had been of us, they would no doubt have continued with us" (1 John 2:19, KJV).
2. **Warnings describe a real danger** (Arminian view): believers who are truly saved can, through persistent unbelief and rebellion, forfeit salvation.
3. **Warnings address the visible community.** Some in the community appear to be Christians without true faith.

## The issue of Hebrews 6
The author's pastoral concern is for the readers' perseverance, and says, "But, beloved, we are persuaded better things of you, and things that accompany salvation" (Hebrews 6:9, KJV). The warning is serious, and the aim is to keep them from drifting.`,
    archaeological: `The recipients of Hebrews and similar warnings faced social and political pressure: loss of property (Hebrews 10:34), imprisonment and the temptation to return to the safety of Judaism or the civil religion of Rome. Edicts against Christians (Nero, Domitian) and the pressure to burn incense to Caesar are documented (Pliny's letter to Trajan, about AD 112, shows Christians being asked to curse Christ, which genuine believers refused). The warnings were not abstract.`,
    apostolic: `The apostles combined confidence with warning. Paul said, "Let him that thinketh he standeth take heed lest he fall" (1 Corinthians 10:12, KJV), and "I keep under my body, and bring it into subjection: lest that by any means... I myself should be a castaway" (1 Corinthians 9:27, KJV). He also wrote, "he which hath begun a good work in you will perform it". Peter urged believers to make their "calling and election sure" (2 Peter 1:10).`,
    prophetic: `Ezekiel 18 and 33 speak of the righteous turning from righteousness, and the wicked turning from wickedness, as a call to repentance and perseverance. God's promise in Jeremiah 32:40, "I will put my fear in their hearts, that they shall not depart from me", shows the new covenant as a work of God that secures perseverance.`,
    theological: `## Common ground
1. Salvation is by grace from first to last.
2. No true believer should live in fear that God will drop them for a stumble; sin in a believer calls for repentance (1 John 1:9).
3. Persistent, unrepentant rejection of Christ leads to condemnation.
4. Believers are called to endure, to watch and to pray.
5. We should not judge individuals' final state; that belongs to God.

## Where they differ
Whether a person truly regenerated by the Spirit can finally fall away. Reformed, Baptist and many evangelical traditions say no; Wesleyan, Arminian and Pentecostal traditions often say yes, though they stress God's power to keep those who trust Him. Catholic and Orthodox churches hold that mortal sin may forfeit grace.`,
    views: `Both camps include great evangelists, missionaries and martyrs. Do not impugn another's faith over this question. Scripture invites both confidence in God's keeping and care in our walk.`,
    pastoral: `For the struggling believer who fears they are lost: if you desire Christ and grieve over sin, that is not the mark of someone abandoned by God. Return to the cross. For the complacent: do not presume. Keep abiding in Christ.` },

  { slug: "women-in-ministry-and-leadership", category: "Church and ministry",
    title: "What does the Bible teach about women in ministry and leadership?",
    summary: "Scripture honours women as prophets, judges, deacons, coworkers and witnesses of the resurrection, and also contains passages (1 Timothy 2:11-15; 1 Corinthians 14:34-35) that many read as restricting teaching authority or elder roles to qualified men. Evangelicals divide into complementarian and egalitarian positions and both seek faithfulness to the text. Every Christian should affirm women's equal worth, gifts and calling to serve.",
    scriptures: ["Genesis 1:26-28", "Judges 4:4-10", "Joel 2:28-29", "Luke 8:1-3", "John 20:11-18", "Acts 2:17-18", "Acts 18:24-26", "Romans 16:1-7", "1 Corinthians 11:2-16", "1 Corinthians 14:33-35", "Galatians 3:26-29", "1 Timothy 2:11-15", "1 Timothy 3:1-13", "Titus 2:3-5"],
    readings: ["Wayne Grudem, Evangelical Feminism and Biblical Truth", "Craig Keener, Paul, Women and Wives", "Two Views on Women in Ministry (Zondervan, Beck and Blomberg eds.)", "Andreas Köstenberger, Women in the Church", "Gordon Fee, Discovering Biblical Equality (essay on 1 Cor 14)"],
    exegetical: `## The foundation
"So God created man in his own image, in the image of God created he him; male and female created he them" (Genesis 1:27, KJV). Both bear God's image and both are given dominion. Galatians 3:28: "There is neither Jew nor Greek... there is neither male nor female: for ye are all one in Christ Jesus" (KJV).

## Women in Scripture
- **Old Testament:** Miriam (Micah 6:4), Deborah the prophet and judge (Judges 4–5), Huldah the prophetess consulted by Josiah (2 Kings 22:14), Esther and Ruth.
- **Jesus and women:** disciples who travelled with Him (Luke 8:1–3), Mary who sat "at Jesus' feet" as a learner (Luke 10:39), the first witnesses of the resurrection (John 20:11–18).
- **Early church:** the daughters of Philip who prophesied (Acts 21:9), Priscilla who with Aquila explained "the way of God more perfectly" to Apollos (Acts 18:26), Phoebe a *diakonos* (servant or deacon) of Cenchrea and *prostatis* (helper) of many (Romans 16:1–2), Junia, "of note among the apostles" (Romans 16:7, a name debated as female or male, with the majority of ancient commentators reading it as female), and the women who prophesied at Corinth (1 Corinthians 11:5).

## The difficult passages
**1 Timothy 2:11–12:** "Let the woman learn in silence with all subjection. But I suffer not a woman to teach, nor to usurp authority over the man, but to be in silence" (KJV). Complementarians read this as a timeless restriction on the teaching and ruling office of elder/overseer, grounded in creation (2:13). Egalitarians read it as a response to a local problem at Ephesus (false teaching, 1 Timothy 1:3–7; 2 Timothy 3:6–7), and note that the verb *authenteō* (G831) is rare and may mean "domineer".

**1 Corinthians 14:34–35:** "Let your women keep silence in the churches". Paul has just allowed women to pray and prophesy (11:5), so the command must be limited, perhaps to disruptive questioning or to the weighing of prophecy. Some manuscripts place the verses elsewhere, and a few scholars regard them as a later addition.

**1 Timothy 3:1–13 and Titus 1:6:** the elder is "the husband of one wife", which complementarians read as a male requirement and egalitarians as a requirement of fidelity. Verse 11 speaks of "women" (or "wives") in the deacon list.

**Ephesians 5:21–33** teaches mutual submission and the husband's sacrificial headship. Both sides discuss the meaning of "head" (*kephalē*).`,
    archaeological: `- **Women leaders in the ancient world.** Inscriptions from Asia Minor and Greece name women as synagogue leaders, benefactors and priestesses. Lydia was a seller of purple (Acts 16:14); inscriptions show that women of means supported religious communities.
- **Early Christian art and inscriptions.** The catacombs of Priscilla in Rome contain early images of women in prayer. Third-century inscriptions record women as deaconesses.
- **Ephesus and the Artemis cult.** Ephesus was dominated by the cult of Artemis with female priestesses, an important background for 1 Timothy 2 according to some interpreters.
- **Pliny's letter (about AD 112)** mentions two enslaved women called *ministrae* ("deaconesses") among the Christians he questioned.`,
    apostolic: `Paul calls many women "fellow workers" (Romans 16:3, Priscilla; 16:6, Mary; 16:12, Tryphena, Tryphosa and Persis; Philippians 4:2–3, Euodia and Syntyche who "laboured with me in the gospel"). Peter on Pentecost cites Joel: "your sons and your daughters shall prophesy" (Acts 2:17). The apostolic church included women in prophecy, prayer, hospitality, teaching and service.`,
    prophetic: `Joel 2:28–29 promises the Spirit on "sons and daughters" and "handmaids". Pentecost fulfils it. Revelation 7 and 14 see all nations and genders before the throne. The prophecy and gifts of the Spirit are not restricted by gender.`,
    theological: `## Common ground
1. Women and men are equal in dignity, in redemption and in the gifts of the Spirit.
2. Women are essential in ministry: teaching women and children, evangelism, mission, intercession, administration, prophecy, counselling, music and service.
3. Abuse and domination of women are sinful; leaders must serve like Christ.
4. Disagreement concerns the offices of elder/pastor and sometimes the teaching of men.

## The two main positions
- **Complementarian:** men and women are equal but have complementary roles; the office of pastor-elder and the main teaching authority is reserved for qualified men. Women serve in many other ways (including as deacons in some churches).
- **Egalitarian:** all offices are open to women according to gifting and calling; the restrictions in the New Testament were local. Many churches (Pentecostal, Methodist, Salvation Army, many Baptist and Anglican groups) have ordained women, citing the Spirit's gifting.

## A way forward
Study the texts together, let gifts be recognised and used, and do not break fellowship. Each church should follow its conviction and decide its practice with humility, honouring those who differ.`,
    views: `The Roman Catholic and Orthodox churches reserve priesthood to men. The Southern Baptist Convention and many Reformed churches are complementarian. Assemblies of God, Church of the Nazarene, Methodists and many Anglican churches ordain women. Pentecostal and charismatic churches have a strong record of women in ministry.`,
    pastoral: `Encourage every believer to discover and use their gifts. Do not let this issue hinder the work of women missionaries, evangelists and teachers. Whatever your church's position, treat women with honour and make sure their gifts are used.` },

  { slug: "speaking-in-tongues-and-spiritual-gifts-today", category: "Church and ministry",
    title: "Speaking in tongues and spiritual gifts: do they continue today?",
    summary: "Pentecostal, charismatic and many other Christians hold that all the gifts of the Spirit continue. Cessationists hold that miraculous gifts such as tongues, prophecy and healing ceased with the apostolic age. Scripture says the gifts are for building up the church, are to be tested and exercised in order and love, and that no gift is a measure of maturity or salvation.",
    scriptures: ["Acts 2:1-13", "Acts 10:44-48", "Acts 19:1-7", "Romans 12:3-8", "1 Corinthians 12:1-31", "1 Corinthians 13:1-13", "1 Corinthians 14:1-40", "Ephesians 4:7-13", "1 Thessalonians 5:19-21", "Joel 2:28-29", "Mark 16:17-18", "Hebrews 2:3-4"],
    readings: ["Gordon Fee, God's Empowering Presence", "Wayne Grudem, Systematic Theology (chapters on gifts)", "Craig Keener, Gift and Giver", "Are Miraculous Gifts for Today? Four Views (Zondervan)", "Jack Deere, Surprised by the Power of the Spirit"],
    exegetical: `## Tongues in Acts
- **Pentecost:** "they were all filled with the Holy Ghost, and began to speak with other tongues, as the Spirit gave them utterance" (Acts 2:4, KJV). The crowd heard "in our own tongues, the wonderful works of God" (2:11), which indicates recognisable languages.
- **Cornelius' household** (Acts 10:44–46) and the **Ephesian disciples** (Acts 19:6) spoke with tongues and magnified God (and prophesied).
- Tongues in Acts signals God's acceptance of new groups (Jews, Gentiles, disciples of John), as the church spread.

## Tongues in 1 Corinthians
- Paul says there are "diversities of gifts, but the same Spirit" (12:4), and the gifts are "given to every man to profit withal" (12:7). Not all speak with tongues (12:30).
- Tongues without interpretation edify the speaker but not the church (14:2–5, 13–19). Paul says, "I thank my God, I speak with tongues more than ye all" (1 Corinthians 14:18, KJV) but "in the church I had rather speak five words with my understanding" (14:19).
- Guidance: two or three at most, in turn, with an interpreter (14:27–28); "Let all things be done decently and in order" (1 Corinthians 14:40, KJV). "Forbid not to speak with tongues" (14:39).
- Love is the greatest: "Though I speak with the tongues of men and of angels, and have not charity, I am become as sounding brass, or a tinkling cymbal" (1 Corinthians 13:1, KJV).

## The cessation argument
1 Corinthians 13:8–10 says that tongues will cease and prophecies will fail "when that which is perfect is come". Cessationists identify "that which is perfect" with the completed canon or the maturing of the church; continuationists see it as Christ's return (the "face to face" of verse 12). Hebrews 2:3–4 speaks of signs confirming the apostles' message. Ephesians 2:20 describes apostles and prophets as the foundation.

## The continuationist argument
Peter at Pentecost cited Joel's promise for "the last days", which continue until Christ returns (Acts 2:16–21; 2:39 "to you, and to your children, and to all that are afar off"). Paul expected gifts to be exercised until "the day of our Lord Jesus Christ" (1 Corinthians 1:7–8). Nothing in the New Testament says that the gifts would end with the apostles. Church history records gifts in the early centuries and in revivals.`,
    archaeological: `- **Corinth.** Excavations show a cosmopolitan port with many languages and mystery cults (Isis, Demeter, Asklepios). Ecstatic speech was known in pagan cults (Delphi), which is probably why Paul insists on order and intelligibility.
- **Early church writings.** Justin Martyr (about AD 150) and Irenaeus (about AD 180) mention spiritual gifts including prophecy and tongues in their churches. The Montanist movement (second century) shows both enthusiasm for the Spirit and the dangers of unregulated prophecy.
- **Modern revivals.** The Azusa Street revival (1906) and the growth of Pentecostal-charismatic Christianity (hundreds of millions today, especially in Africa, Latin America and Asia) are historical facts. Their evaluation is theological.`,
    apostolic: `Paul's teaching shows that gifts are to build up the church (1 Corinthians 14:12, 26), and are to be tested: "Prove all things; hold fast that which is good" (1 Thessalonians 5:21, KJV). He says, "Quench not the Spirit. Despise not prophesyings" (5:19–20, KJV). John says to "try the spirits whether they are of God" (1 John 4:1). Peter instructs, "As every man hath received the gift, even so minister the same one to another" (1 Peter 4:10, KJV).`,
    prophetic: `The promise of Joel 2:28–29 and Numbers 11:29 (Moses: "Would God that all the LORD's people were prophets") sees the Spirit given widely. The gift of prophecy in the New Testament is to be tested and subordinate to Scripture and apostolic teaching. Revelation 19:10 says "the testimony of Jesus is the spirit of prophecy", so all prophecy should exalt Christ.`,
    theological: `## Principles accepted by all
1. **The Holy Spirit is God** and gives gifts as He wills (1 Corinthians 12:11).
2. **Scripture is the final authority.** No tongue, prophecy or vision is added to the canon.
3. **Fruit before gifts.** Love is the "more excellent way" (12:31). Gifts without character are dangerous (Matthew 7:21–23).
4. **Order and edification.** Public use of gifts must serve others.
5. **No second-class Christians.** All who believe have the Spirit (Romans 8:9). Speaking in tongues is not a requirement for salvation or proof of maturity.
6. **Test everything** and beware counterfeits and manipulation.

## Views on the "baptism in the Spirit"
- **Pentecostal:** a distinct experience after conversion, evidenced by tongues (Assemblies of God).
- **Charismatic / Third Wave:** a deeper filling or empowerment, with various evidences.
- **Reformed and most evangelicals:** Spirit baptism occurs at conversion (1 Corinthians 12:13), with repeated fillings (Ephesians 5:18).
- **Cessationist:** the miraculous gifts ended; God still heals and answers prayer.`,
    views: `Pentecostal denominations (Assemblies of God, Church of God in Christ, Redeemed Christian Church of God, Apostolic Faith) are continuationist; the Southern Baptist, Presbyterian and Reformed churches vary; Catholic charismatic renewal accepts gifts; Dispensational groups often hold cessationism. Respect each other's convictions.`,
    pastoral: `Where gifts are practised, teach order, love and accountability. Where they are not, stay open to the Spirit's work and avoid mocking. Never make any gift a test of spiritual status. Seek the Giver more than the gifts.` },

  { slug: "sabbath-saturday-or-sunday", category: "Church and ministry",
    title: "Sabbath or Sunday? Which day should Christians worship?",
    summary: "The Sabbath command is part of the Ten Commandments and was a sign of the Mosaic covenant. The New Testament shows believers meeting on the first day (Sunday) in honour of the resurrection, and Paul teaches that no one may judge another about 'sabbath days'. Christians differ: most hold the Lord's Day replaces the Sabbath as a day of worship and rest; Seventh-day Adventists and some others keep the seventh day; others see the Sabbath fulfilled in Christ.",
    scriptures: ["Genesis 2:2-3", "Exodus 20:8-11", "Exodus 31:12-17", "Deuteronomy 5:12-15", "Isaiah 58:13-14", "Mark 2:23-28", "Acts 20:7", "1 Corinthians 16:1-2", "Romans 14:5-6", "Colossians 2:16-17", "Hebrews 4:1-11", "Revelation 1:10"],
    readings: ["D. A. Carson (ed.), From Sabbath to Lord's Day", "Skip MacCarty, In Granite or Ingrained?", "Andrew Lincoln, Paradise Now and Not Yet", "Samuele Bacchiocchi, From Sabbath to Sunday (Adventist perspective)", "Four Views on the Role of Works"],
    exegetical: `## The Sabbath in the Old Testament
God rested on the seventh day and "blessed the seventh day, and sanctified it" (Genesis 2:3, KJV). The fourth commandment: "Remember the sabbath day, to keep it holy... in it thou shalt not do any work... For in six days the LORD made heaven and earth... and rested the seventh day" (Exodus 20:8–11, KJV). Deuteronomy 5:15 links it to redemption from Egypt. It was "a sign between me and the children of Israel for ever" (Exodus 31:17).

## Jesus and the Sabbath
Jesus healed on the Sabbath and said, "The sabbath was made for man, and not man for the sabbath: Therefore the Son of man is Lord also of the sabbath" (Mark 2:27–28, KJV). He fulfilled the law without abolishing it (Matthew 5:17), and offers rest to the weary (Matthew 11:28).

## The first day
- Jesus rose on "the first day of the week" (Matthew 28:1; John 20:1) and appeared to disciples that evening and a week later (John 20:19, 26).
- Pentecost fell on the first day (counting from the Sabbath after Passover).
- "And upon the first day of the week, when the disciples came together to break bread, Paul preached unto them" (Acts 20:7, KJV).
- "Upon the first day of the week let every one of you lay by him in store, as God hath prospered him" (1 Corinthians 16:2, KJV).
- John was "in the Spirit on the Lord's day" (Revelation 1:10, KJV), probably Sunday.

## Paul on days
"One man esteemeth one day above another: another esteemeth every day alike. Let every man be fully persuaded in his own mind" (Romans 14:5, KJV). "Let no man therefore judge you in meat, or in drink, or in respect of an holyday, or of the new moon, or of the sabbath days: Which are a shadow of things to come; but the body is of Christ" (Colossians 2:16–17, KJV). Galatians 4:10–11 criticises observing "days, and months, and times, and years".

## Hebrews 4
"There remaineth therefore a rest to the people of God" (Hebrews 4:9, KJV) uses *sabbatismos*, a sabbath rest, which believers enter by faith in Christ and which will be consummated in the age to come.`,
    archaeological: `- **Jewish Sabbath observance** was well known in the Roman world. Josephus, Philo and Roman authors (Seneca, Juvenal, Tacitus) mention it, often critically. The Elephantine papyri (5th century BC) mention a Sabbath. First-century Jewish Sabbath practice included synagogue reading, as shown in Luke 4:16 and by synagogue remains at Capernaum, Gamla and Magdala.
- **Early Christian worship.** Pliny's letter (about AD 112) says that Christians met "on a fixed day" before dawn. The Didache (about AD 100) speaks of gathering "on the Lord's day of the Lord". Ignatius (about AD 110) mentions "living no longer for the Sabbath but for the Lord's Day". Justin Martyr (about AD 155) describes Sunday worship in Rome.
- **Constantine's decree (AD 321)** made Sunday a day of rest in the empire, but Sunday worship was already widespread; the claim that Constantine invented Sunday worship is not supported by the early evidence.`,
    apostolic: `The apostolic council (Acts 15) did not require Gentile believers to keep the Mosaic law, and the Sabbath is not mentioned among the requirements. Paul assumed Gentile believers would not be judged on it. He kept Jewish customs when evangelising Jews (Acts 18:4; 1 Corinthians 9:20), meeting in synagogues on the Sabbath.`,
    prophetic: `Isaiah 66:23 promises "from one sabbath to another, shall all flesh come to worship before me". The theme of rest is eschatological: the final Sabbath is the new creation (Hebrews 4:9–11; Revelation 14:13). The Lord's Day is a weekly foretaste.`,
    theological: `## Positions
1. **Lord's Day (Sunday Sabbath).** The principle of one day in seven for rest and worship is permanent, and the day changed to Sunday in honour of the resurrection (Westminster Confession).
2. **Seventh-day Sabbath.** The fourth commandment stands; Saturday is the day (Seventh-day Adventists, Seventh Day Baptists, Messianic Jews).
3. **Fulfilled in Christ.** The Sabbath was a shadow, now fulfilled in Christ; Christians worship on Sunday by custom and freedom, and rest in Christ daily (many Lutherans, Dispensationalists and others).

## Shared values
- A weekly rhythm of rest and worship is a gift for body and soul.
- The church must gather regularly: "Not forsaking the assembling of ourselves together" (Hebrews 10:25, KJV).
- Do not condemn one another on disputable matters (Romans 14).
- Do not use the Sabbath to claim others are unsaved.`,
    views: `Seventh-day Adventists and Seventh Day Baptists keep Saturday. Most other churches worship on Sunday. Jewish believers in Jesus often keep Saturday and worship together on Sunday. The Eastern and Western churches share Sunday worship from the earliest period.`,
    pastoral: `Commend the practice of setting aside time for rest and worship, in love and freedom. In regions where Sunday work is required, believers can set aside another time. Avoid dividing the church over days.` },

  { slug: "tithing-and-giving-in-the-new-testament", category: "Church and ministry",
    title: "Is tithing required for Christians today?",
    summary: "The tithe was part of Israel's law (about a tenth to support Levites, the festivals and the poor). The New Testament never commands Christians to tithe, yet commends generous, regular, proportionate, cheerful giving to support ministers and the poor. Many churches teach the tithe as a helpful minimum; others call for giving beyond the tithe. Manipulation or promises of guaranteed wealth for giving are not biblical.",
    scriptures: ["Genesis 14:18-20", "Genesis 28:20-22", "Leviticus 27:30-32", "Numbers 18:21-24", "Deuteronomy 14:22-29", "Malachi 3:8-12", "Matthew 23:23", "1 Corinthians 9:1-14", "1 Corinthians 16:1-2", "2 Corinthians 8:1-15", "2 Corinthians 9:6-8", "Acts 4:32-37"],
    readings: ["Craig Blomberg, Neither Poverty nor Riches", "Randy Alcorn, Money, Possessions, and Eternity", "Christopher Wright, Old Testament Ethics for the People of God", "David Croteau, You Mean I Don't Have to Tithe?", "Russell Earl Kelly, Should the Church Teach Tithing?"],
    exegetical: `## Tithing before the law
Abraham gave Melchizedek "tithes of all" (Genesis 14:20), and Jacob vowed to give a tenth (Genesis 28:22). These are voluntary acts, not commands.

## Tithing under the law
Leviticus 27:30: "all the tithe of the land... is the LORD's". Numbers 18:21 assigns the tithe to the Levites, who had no land inheritance. Deuteronomy 14:22–29 provides for a festival tithe and a third-year tithe for the Levite, the stranger, the fatherless and the widow. In practice Israel gave more than 10% overall (tithes, offerings, firstfruits, gleaning). Malachi 3:8–10 accuses the people of robbing God "in tithes and offerings" and invites them to "bring ye all the tithes into the storehouse".

## Jesus
He said to the Pharisees, "ye pay tithe of mint and anise and cummin, and have omitted the weightier matters of the law, judgment, mercy, and faith: these ought ye to have done, and not to leave the other undone" (Matthew 23:23, KJV). He affirmed tithing as part of the law still in force at the time, while stressing justice and mercy. He praised the widow who gave her all (Mark 12:41–44) and told the rich young man to sell everything (Mark 10:21).

## The apostles
- The early church shared possessions generously (Acts 2:44–45; 4:32–35).
- Paul said that "they which preach the gospel should live of the gospel" (1 Corinthians 9:14, KJV).
- Giving is to be regular and proportionate: "Upon the first day of the week let every one of you lay by him in store, as God hath prospered him" (1 Corinthians 16:2, KJV).
- The Macedonians gave "beyond their power" (2 Corinthians 8:3).
- "Every man according as he purposeth in his heart, so let him give; not grudgingly, or of necessity: for God loveth a cheerful giver" (2 Corinthians 9:7, KJV).
- No New Testament passage instructs Gentile Christians to tithe. Hebrews 7 discusses the tithe in the context of Melchizedek's priesthood, not as a command.

## Malachi 3 and curses
Malachi addressed Israel under the covenant; the "curse" (3:9) is the covenant curse of Deuteronomy 28. Applying this to Christians who are in Christ, who bore the curse for us (Galatians 3:13), requires care.`,
    archaeological: `- **Tithing in the ancient Near East.** Tithes to temples and kings are recorded in Mesopotamia (Ur III and Babylonian texts), Ugarit and Phoenician sites such as Carthage; the practice was widespread. Israel's tithe was distinctive in supporting the Levites, the poor and the festival.
- **Jar handles inscribed "to the king" (lmlk) and storehouse remains** from Judah illustrate the collection of taxes and tithes in kind.
- **Early Christian practice.** Writers from the 2nd century (Justin, Tertullian) describe voluntary offerings brought on Sunday for the poor, widows, orphans and prisoners. Systematic tithing in the church developed from the 4th to 6th centuries.`,
    apostolic: `The apostles' focus was on generosity, sharing and support of ministers and the poor. Paul organised the collection for the poor in Jerusalem (Romans 15:25–27; 1 Corinthians 16; 2 Corinthians 8–9). James insists that faith care for the poor (James 2:15–16). John asks, "whoso hath this world's good, and seeth his brother have need, and shutteth up his bowels of compassion from him, how dwelleth the love of God in him?" (1 John 3:17, KJV).`,
    prophetic: `The prophets link proper worship with justice for the poor (Isaiah 58; Amos 5; Micah 6:6–8). The test of true giving is the heart. The messianic hope includes generosity, as in the church of Acts.`,
    theological: `## Principles
1. **Everything belongs to God**; we are stewards (Psalm 24:1).
2. **Giving is worship** and an act of faith and love, not a transaction to "buy" blessings.
3. **The tithe is a useful guide** that many Christians find helpful as a starting point, while the New Testament standard is generous, sacrificial and cheerful giving.
4. **Support of ministry and the poor** are both biblical priorities.
5. **Accountability.** Churches must handle money transparently and honestly (2 Corinthians 8:20–21).

## Abuses to avoid
- Promising wealth in return for "seed" offerings.
- Pressure or threats of curses.
- Leaders enriching themselves (1 Timothy 3:3; 6:5–10; 1 Peter 5:2).
- Burdening the poor while the leaders live in luxury.`,
    views: `Some teach that the tithe is binding and should be paid to the local church; some that the tithe is the minimum and offerings are beyond it; others that the New Testament standard is voluntary, proportionate giving; others that all we have is God's. Most agree on generosity, transparency and care for the poor.`,
    pastoral: `Teach giving as joyful worship and trust in God. Remind leaders that money is a stewardship. Care for the poor and do not shame them for their inability to give.` },

  { slug: "rapture-tribulation-and-millennium", category: "Prophecy and the end",
    title: "The rapture, the tribulation and the millennium: what does the Bible teach?",
    summary: "All orthodox Christians agree that Christ will return visibly and in glory, that the dead will be raised, that there will be judgement and that God will make all things new. They differ on the order of events: whether the church is taken up before, during or after a period of tribulation, and whether the thousand years of Revelation 20 is literal, future, present or symbolic. Charitable humility is required.",
    scriptures: ["Matthew 24:1-44", "Daniel 9:24-27", "John 14:1-3", "1 Corinthians 15:50-58", "1 Thessalonians 4:13-18", "2 Thessalonians 2:1-12", "Revelation 7:9-17", "Revelation 20:1-15", "Revelation 21:1-5", "Romans 11:25-27"],
    readings: ["The Meaning of the Millennium: Four Views (IVP, Clouse ed.)", "Three Views on the Rapture (Zondervan)", "Sam Storms, Kingdom Come", "George Eldon Ladd, The Blessed Hope", "Craig Blaising and Darrell Bock, Progressive Dispensationalism", "Kim Riddlebarger, A Case for Amillennialism"],
    exegetical: `## The "rapture"
"For the Lord himself shall descend from heaven with a shout, with the voice of the archangel, and with the trump of God: and the dead in Christ shall rise first: Then we which are alive and remain shall be caught up together with them in the clouds, to meet the Lord in the air" (1 Thessalonians 4:16–17, KJV). The Latin *rapiemur* ("caught up") gives the word "rapture". The Greek is *harpazō*. Believers are gathered to Christ. Paul adds: "We shall not all sleep, but we shall all be changed, In a moment, in the twinkling of an eye, at the last trump" (1 Corinthians 15:51–52, KJV).

## The tribulation
Jesus spoke of "great tribulation, such as was not since the beginning of the world to this time" (Matthew 24:21, KJV). Daniel 9:24–27 outlines seventy "weeks" with the final week of confirmed covenant and "abomination". Revelation 6–19 describes seals, trumpets and bowls. Paul describes a "falling away" and "the man of sin" (2 Thessalonians 2:3).

## The millennium
Revelation 20:1–6 speaks of Satan bound a thousand years, martyrs who "lived and reigned with Christ a thousand years", and "the first resurrection". It is the only passage that mentions a thousand-year reign.

## Main views
1. **Premillennial, pretribulation (dispensational).** Christ returns secretly for the church before a seven-year tribulation; Christ then returns with the church to reign for a literal thousand years. Israel plays a distinct role. This view became popular through J. N. Darby (1830s) and the Scofield Bible.
2. **Premillennial, posttribulation (historic).** The church goes through the tribulation; Christ returns visibly, the church meets Him and returns with Him to reign a thousand years. Held by many in the early church (Papias, Irenaeus, Justin Martyr).
3. **Midtribulation and prewrath.** The church is taken up at the middle or toward the end of the tribulation.
4. **Amillennial.** The thousand years is the present church age (Satan restricted from deceiving the nations, saints reigning with Christ in heaven); Christ returns once, the dead are raised and judged, and the new creation follows. Held by Augustine, Aquinas, the Reformers and many Reformed, Lutheran, Catholic and Orthodox Christians.
5. **Postmillennial.** The gospel will bring a golden age before Christ returns. Held by many Puritans and 19th-century revivalists.
6. **Preterist and idealist readings** treat much of Matthew 24 and Revelation as fulfilled in AD 70 or as symbolic of the whole age.`,
    archaeological: `- **Jewish apocalyptic writing.** The Dead Sea Scrolls (the War Scroll), 1 Enoch, 4 Ezra and 2 Baruch show the type of literature that shaped expectations at the time of Jesus: visions, symbols, cosmic conflict and final victory.
- **Daniel's context.** Babylonian and Persian court tablets and the Nabonidus Chronicle illustrate Daniel's setting. The fourth-kingdom and "little horn" identifications (Rome, Antiochus IV or a future ruler) divide interpreters.
- **Rome and the Temple.** The destruction of the Temple in AD 70 (Josephus; Arch of Titus) is the historical reference point for Luke 21:20–24 and parts of the Olivet Discourse.
- **Early church writings.** Papias, Justin and Irenaeus expected a literal earthly kingdom; Origen and Augustine promoted a spiritual reading, shaping Catholic and Reformation teaching.`,
    apostolic: `The apostles taught that Christ's return is the hope of the church: "Looking for that blessed hope, and the glorious appearing of the great God and our Saviour Jesus Christ" (Titus 2:13, KJV). Their emphasis was on readiness: "Watch therefore: for ye know not what hour your Lord doth come" (Matthew 24:42, KJV). Paul wrote, "comfort one another with these words" (1 Thessalonians 4:18, KJV), and Peter, "what manner of persons ought ye to be in all holy conversation and godliness" (2 Peter 3:11, KJV).`,
    prophetic: `The prophetic books promise the Day of the LORD, the restoration of Israel, the kingdom of the Messiah, the nations coming to Zion, and the new heavens and earth (Isaiah 2; 11; 65; Ezekiel 37–48; Daniel 2, 7; Zechariah 12–14). The New Testament sees their fulfilment in Christ, partly now and fully at His return. Paul says "all Israel shall be saved" (Romans 11:26), debated as to its meaning.`,
    theological: `## Central agreement
1. Christ will return personally, visibly and gloriously.
2. The dead will be raised, the righteous to eternal life and the wicked to judgement.
3. Evil will be judged and removed, and the new creation will come.
4. We should live in readiness, holiness, witness and hope.

## Cautions
- **Date-setting** is forbidden: "But of that day and hour knoweth no man" (Matthew 24:36). Many predicted dates have failed.
- **Fear-based sensationalism** and headlines-chasing distort the message.
- **Political identifications** of antichrist with a nation or leader have repeatedly been wrong.
- **Division over the order of events** should not break fellowship.`,
    views: `Pretribulation premillennialism is popular among Baptists, Pentecostals and independent evangelicals; amillennialism among Reformed, Lutheran, Catholic and Orthodox churches; historic premillennialism among some Baptists and Anglicans; postmillennialism among some Reformed groups. All affirm the return of Christ.`,
    pastoral: `Emphasise the hope: Christ is coming. Teach people to live faithfully now, to share the gospel, to comfort the grieving (1 Thessalonians 4:18) and to stay awake. Do not let charts replace Christ.` },

  { slug: "the-antichrist-and-666", category: "Prophecy and the end",
    title: "Who is the Antichrist? What is the meaning of 666?",
    summary: "The word 'antichrist' appears only in John's letters, where it describes both a coming figure and the many who deny the Father and the Son. Revelation 13 speaks of a beast whose number is 666, widely linked in the first century to Nero (by Hebrew letter-values), and read by others as a symbol of imperfect human power or a future ruler. Christians have repeatedly misidentified him; the Bible's purpose is to warn and strengthen, not to feed speculation.",
    scriptures: ["Daniel 7:7-8", "Daniel 9:26-27", "Matthew 24:15", "2 Thessalonians 2:1-12", "1 John 2:18-22", "1 John 4:1-3", "2 John 1:7", "Revelation 13:1-18", "Revelation 17:1-18", "Revelation 19:19-20"],
    readings: ["G. K. Beale, The Book of Revelation (NIGTC)", "Craig Keener, Revelation (NIV Application Commentary)", "Richard Bauckham, The Climax of Prophecy", "Leon Morris, The Revelation of St. John"],
    exegetical: `## The word
*Antichristos* (G500) means "against Christ" or "instead of Christ". It occurs five times: 1 John 2:18, 22; 4:3; 2 John 7. John writes: "Little children, it is the last time: and as ye have heard that antichrist shall come, even now are there many antichrists" (1 John 2:18, KJV). The test: "Who is a liar but he that denieth that Jesus is the Christ? He is antichrist, that denieth the Father and the Son" (1 John 2:22, KJV). The spirit of antichrist "even now already is in the world" (1 John 4:3).

## Related figures
- **The "man of sin"** (2 Thessalonians 2:3–10): "the son of perdition; Who opposeth and exalteth himself above all that is called God, or that is worshipped; so that he as God sitteth in the temple of God" (KJV). He is revealed after "a falling away" and is destroyed by the Lord's coming. Paul says "the mystery of iniquity doth already work" (2:7).
- **The "little horn"** of Daniel 7:8, 24–25 and **the "abomination of desolation"** (Daniel 9:27; 11:31; 12:11), partly fulfilled by Antiochus IV in 167 BC (1 Maccabees 1:54), and cited by Jesus as future from his perspective (Matthew 24:15).
- **The beast from the sea and the beast from the land** (Revelation 13): a political power blasphemously claiming worship, and a religious power promoting it.

## The number 666
"Here is wisdom. Let him that hath understanding count the number of the beast: for it is the number of a man; and his number is Six hundred threescore and six" (Revelation 13:18, KJV). Interpretations:
1. **Nero.** By gematria (letters as numbers), the Hebrew spelling of "Nero Caesar" (נרון קסר) totals 666, and some manuscripts read 616, matching the Latin spelling "Nero Caesar". Nero persecuted Christians, and a legend said he would return.
2. **A symbol of imperfection.** Seven is the number of completeness, so 666 falls repeatedly short: a human power that imitates God's perfection.
3. **A future individual.** Many futurist interpreters expect a literal world ruler.
4. **The Roman Empire or any system** that demands ultimate loyalty.
The "mark" (13:16–17) is a mark of allegiance on the right hand and forehead, in contrast to the seal of God on His servants (7:3; 14:1).`,
    archaeological: `- **Nero.** Coins from the empire name "Nero Caesar" with the title "God" (*theos*) in some eastern cities; Nero's persecution of Christians (AD 64) is recorded by Tacitus (*Annals* 15.44) and Suetonius. A wall graffito from Pompeii and other graffiti show that gematria was a popular game ("I love her whose number is 545").
- **Papyrus manuscripts of Revelation.** P115 (3rd century) and Codex Ephraemi read 616 in Revelation 13:18, which supports the Nero gematria with the Latin spelling.
- **Emperor worship.** Temples to Roma and the emperors in Pergamum, Ephesus, Smyrna and elsewhere, and the use of imperial coinage and inscriptions that proclaim the emperor "lord and god", form the context for Revelation's beast and mark.
- **Domitian** is said by the historian Suetonius to have required to be addressed as "lord and god".`,
    apostolic: `John's pastoral purpose is to protect believers from deceivers: "Beloved, believe not every spirit, but try the spirits whether they are of God" (1 John 4:1, KJV). Paul warns that "the Lord shall consume with the spirit of his mouth, and shall destroy with the brightness of his coming" the lawless one (2 Thessalonians 2:8, KJV). The answer to antichrist is to hold fast to the apostolic gospel.`,
    prophetic: `The pattern is that proud empires and rulers claim divine honours and are judged: Babylon (Daniel 4), Antiochus IV, Rome. Revelation 19:19–20 shows the beast and the false prophet defeated by Christ at His return. The prophecies are meant to strengthen the faithful: "Here is the patience and the faith of the saints" (Revelation 13:10, KJV).`,
    theological: `## Four readings in brief
- **Preterist:** Nero and the first-century church.
- **Historicist:** the Papacy (Reformation view; now less widely held).
- **Futurist:** a personal ruler at the end of the age.
- **Idealist:** a recurring pattern of anti-Christian power.

## Practical wisdom
1. **Do not name individuals.** Over the centuries, people have called Nero, Muhammad, popes, Napoleon, Hitler, Stalin and others the antichrist.
2. **Do not fear technology.** Barcodes, chips, vaccines and cards are not the mark of the beast by themselves. The mark is about worship and allegiance.
3. **Fear God, not the beast.** The Lamb conquers.
4. **Test teachings.** The true mark of antichrist is denying that Jesus is the Christ come in the flesh.
5. **Live faithfully.** Revelation's call is to endurance, worship and witness.`,
    views: `Futurist, preterist, historicist and idealist interpreters can all be sincere believers. Historic Protestants once identified the pope with the Antichrist; most evangelicals today take a more cautious view. Catholic and Orthodox traditions expect a future adversary before Christ's return.`,
    pastoral: `When believers are anxious about the mark or the end times, direct them to the Lord's promises and to John's test of the spirits. Encourage love, prayer and mission.` },

  { slug: "prosperity-gospel-wealth-and-faith", category: "Church and ministry",
    title: "Is the prosperity gospel biblical? What does the Bible teach about wealth?",
    summary: "The Bible celebrates God's generosity and blessing, and teaches that wealth is a gift to be used with gratitude, honesty and generosity. It also warns that the love of money is a root of all evil, that suffering is part of discipleship, and that God does not guarantee earthly riches for faith or gifts. Teaching that treats God as a cosmic vending machine, or measures spirituality by wealth or health, contradicts the New Testament.",
    scriptures: ["Deuteronomy 8:11-18", "Proverbs 10:22", "Proverbs 30:7-9", "Malachi 3:10", "Matthew 6:19-34", "Mark 10:17-31", "Luke 12:13-21", "2 Corinthians 8:9", "Philippians 4:11-13", "1 Timothy 6:3-19", "3 John 1:2", "James 5:1-6", "Revelation 3:14-22"],
    readings: ["Gordon Fee, The Disease of the Health and Wealth Gospels", "Craig Blomberg, Neither Poverty nor Riches", "Kate Bowler, Blessed: A History of the American Prosperity Gospel", "Randy Alcorn, The Treasure Principle", "Paul Gifford, Ghana's New Christianity"],
    exegetical: `## What the Bible says in favour of blessing
God blesses the obedient (Deuteronomy 28:1–14). "The blessing of the LORD, it maketh rich, and he addeth no sorrow with it" (Proverbs 10:22, KJV). Abraham, Job, Solomon and others were wealthy. Jesus said He came that we "might have life, and that [we] might have it more abundantly" (John 10:10). Paul taught that "God shall supply all your need according to his riches in glory by Christ Jesus" (Philippians 4:19, KJV). God is the giver of "every good gift" (James 1:17).

## What it also says
- **Wealth is dangerous.** "For the love of money is the root of all evil: which while some coveted after, they have erred from the faith, and pierced themselves through with many sorrows" (1 Timothy 6:10, KJV). "Take heed, and beware of covetousness: for a man's life consisteth not in the abundance of the things which he possesseth" (Luke 12:15, KJV). "How hardly shall they that have riches enter into the kingdom of God!" (Mark 10:23, KJV).
- **God's people suffer.** Jesus: "In the world ye shall have tribulation" (John 16:33). Paul: "all that will live godly in Christ Jesus shall suffer persecution" (2 Timothy 3:12, KJV). He had learned "in whatsoever state I am, therewith to be content" (Philippians 4:11). Job's friends were wrong to assume that suffering means sin.
- **The poor are loved.** "Hath not God chosen the poor of this world rich in faith?" (James 2:5, KJV). Jesus was born in a manger and said, "the Son of man hath not where to lay his head" (Matthew 8:20).
- **Warnings to the church.** To Laodicea, who said "I am rich, and increased with goods", Christ said, "thou art wretched, and miserable, and poor, and blind, and naked" (Revelation 3:17, KJV). Smyrna was "poor" but "rich" (Revelation 2:9).

## Texts used by prosperity teachers
- **2 Corinthians 8:9:** "though he was rich, yet for your sakes he became poor, that ye through his poverty might be rich" (KJV). In context Paul is urging generosity, and the riches are spiritual.
- **3 John 2:** "I wish above all things that thou mayest prosper and be in health" (KJV) is a personal greeting, like "I hope you are well".
- **Mark 11:24 and John 14:13–14:** promises of prayer are to be read in light of "in my name", God's will (1 John 5:14) and Jesus' own prayer in Gethsemane.
- **Galatians 3:13–14:** the blessing of Abraham comes to Gentiles "through faith" and is the Spirit (3:14), not necessarily material wealth.
- **Malachi 3:10** addressed Israel (see the question on tithing).`,
    archaeological: `- **Wealth and poverty in the first century.** Most people in the Roman world lived near subsistence. Excavations show the contrast between the villas of Herodian Jerusalem and the simple homes of Galilee. Jesus' ministry was largely among peasants.
- **Corinth and Laodicea.** Laodicea was a banking and textile centre that was so wealthy it refused imperial aid after the earthquake of AD 60 (Tacitus), which illuminates Revelation 3:17. Excavations at Corinth show a divided society, with the wealthy dining in temple dining rooms; Paul addressed abuses in the Lord's Supper (1 Corinthians 11).
- **Modern history.** The prosperity message arose in 20th-century America (Kenneth Hagin, Oral Roberts, Kenneth Copeland) from the "Word of Faith" movement and spread worldwide, including Africa, where it has both attracted millions and been criticised by many Christian leaders.`,
    apostolic: `The apostles worked, gave and suffered. Paul worked as a tentmaker (Acts 18:3), refused to be a burden (1 Thessalonians 2:9), and listed his sufferings (2 Corinthians 11:23–28). Peter said, "Silver and gold have I none; but such as I have give I thee" (Acts 3:6, KJV). James denounced those who exploit workers (5:1–6). John taught believers to love in "deed and in truth" (1 John 3:18).`,
    prophetic: `The prophets denounced greed and injustice (Amos 2:6–7; Micah 2:1–2; Isaiah 5:8). They promised the restoration of God's people, including material abundance, but linked it to righteousness (Isaiah 58:6–10). The final fulfilment is in the new creation, where there are no tears.`,
    theological: `## A balanced view
1. **God is generous and wants to bless His people.** We can pray for provision, healing and success without shame.
2. **Faith is trust in God, not a force to control Him.** He answers according to His wisdom (1 John 5:14).
3. **God is the Lord of suffering too.** Faithful people can be poor, sick or persecuted.
4. **Money is a stewardship.** Work hard, be honest, give generously, and care for the poor.
5. **True riches are in Christ.** "Godliness with contentment is great gain" (1 Timothy 6:6).
6. **Leaders must be above reproach** in finances.

## Discernment questions
- Is Christ at the centre or the money?
- Is the teaching consistent with the whole Bible?
- Are the poor and the suffering treated with dignity?
- Are leaders accountable and humble?`,
    views: `Prosperity teaching is rejected as heresy by some critics and defended by its advocates as a recovery of biblical promises. A moderate position, held by many evangelical and Pentecostal leaders, affirms that God blesses and provides while denying that faith or giving guarantees wealth. The Lausanne Movement's 2010 Cape Town Commitment criticised the prosperity gospel.`,
    pastoral: `Encourage people to work, save, give and trust God. Comfort the poor and the sick with Christ's presence, not guilt. Encourage leaders to practise financial integrity and simplicity. Preach contentment and generosity.` },

  { slug: "are-there-apostles-and-prophets-today", category: "Church and ministry",
    title: "Are there apostles and prophets today?",
    summary: "The New Testament uses 'apostle' in a narrow sense (the Twelve, Paul, eyewitnesses of the risen Christ who laid the church's foundation) and a broader sense (messengers or church planters such as Barnabas, Silas, Andronicus and Junia). Christians differ on whether the broader ministry continues. All agree that there are no new apostles who add to Scripture, and that every prophecy must be tested.",
    scriptures: ["Matthew 10:1-4", "Acts 1:21-26", "1 Corinthians 15:5-9", "Ephesians 2:19-22", "Ephesians 3:4-5", "Ephesians 4:7-16", "1 Corinthians 12:28", "Acts 14:4", "Romans 16:7", "1 Thessalonians 2:6", "Revelation 21:14", "1 Corinthians 14:29-32", "1 John 4:1"],
    readings: ["Wayne Grudem, The Gift of Prophecy in the New Testament and Today", "Don Carson, Showing the Spirit", "Craig Keener, Spirit Hermeneutics", "Ronald Fung, essay on Ephesians 4", "Andrew Wilson, Spirit and Sacrament (on charismatic theology)", "Francis Chan and others on church planting"],
    exegetical: `## "Apostle" in the New Testament
*Apostolos* (G652) means "one sent".
**The Twelve** were chosen by Jesus (Matthew 10:2–4) and are the foundation of the new Jerusalem (Revelation 21:14). Matthias replaced Judas on the condition of having been with Jesus from His baptism until the ascension and being "a witness with us of his resurrection" (Acts 1:21–22, KJV).
**Paul** was an apostle "born out of due time" who saw the risen Lord (1 Corinthians 15:8–9), appointed directly by Christ.
**James**, the Lord's brother (Galatians 1:19), was also called an apostle.
**Others** are called apostles in a looser sense: Barnabas (Acts 14:4, 14), Silvanus and Timothy (1 Thessalonians 2:6), Andronicus and Junia (Romans 16:7), Apollos (1 Corinthians 4:6, 9), and messengers of the churches (2 Corinthians 8:23; Philippians 2:25, *apostolos* translated "messenger").

## Ephesians 4:11–13
"And he gave some, apostles; and some, prophets; and some, evangelists; and some, pastors and teachers; For the perfecting of the saints, for the work of the ministry, for the edifying of the body of Christ: Till we all come in the unity of the faith" (KJV). Cessationists say that apostles and prophets were foundational (Ephesians 2:20) and have ceased, while the others continue. Continuationists argue that the list is a set of gifts to the church "till we all come" to maturity, which has not yet happened.

## Prophets
The New Testament prophet speaks "unto men to edification, and exhortation, and comfort" (1 Corinthians 14:3, KJV). New Testament prophecy is to be "judged" by others (14:29), and Agabus and Philip's daughters prophesied (Acts 11:28; 21:9–11). Old Testament prophets spoke with absolute authority, and false prophecy was punished by death (Deuteronomy 18:20–22). Many scholars see a difference in New Testament prophecy: it is a Spirit-prompted word that is to be tested and is subordinate to Scripture (Grudem), although some hold it to be the same as that of the Old Testament.`,
    archaeological: `- **The Didache** (late first or early second century) shows itinerant "apostles and prophets" still active in churches and gives tests: an apostle should not stay more than two days; a prophet who asks for money in the Spirit is a false prophet. This shows that the broader ministries persisted in the generation after the apostles, together with the problem of abuse.
- **Montanism** (c. AD 160) shows the dangers of unregulated prophecy and the church's insistence that new revelation be tested against apostolic teaching.
- **Missionary movements.** Church history, from Patrick and Boniface to William Carey, Hudson Taylor and African church planters, shows people functioning as "sent ones" (missionary apostles) and prophets (reformers and revivalists calling the church back to God).`,
    apostolic: `The apostles considered their authority unique: they were eyewitnesses (Acts 1:22), commissioned directly by Christ and had power for signs (2 Corinthians 12:12). Paul says that the church is "built upon the foundation of the apostles and prophets, Jesus Christ himself being the chief corner stone" (Ephesians 2:20, KJV). A foundation is laid once. At the same time, Paul passes on the work to faithful men (2 Timothy 2:2) and calls church planters his "fellow workers".`,
    prophetic: `Joel 2:28–29 and Acts 2:17–18 expect prophecy among the people of God in the last days. Revelation 11:3 mentions two witnesses who prophesy. In every age God raises people to proclaim His word. The test of a prophet is faithfulness to the revealed word (Isaiah 8:20), accurate fulfilment where predictions are made, and moral character ("By their fruits ye shall know them", Matthew 7:20).`,
    theological: `## Common ground
1. The office of apostle in the foundational sense (witness of the risen Christ with unique authority) is closed. No one today can add to Scripture.
2. God still sends people to plant churches, pioneer new regions and lead the church (the missionary-apostolic function).
3. God still speaks to His people by His Spirit, and the church should welcome and test prophetic words.
4. Titles are less important than faithfulness and humility. Jesus said, "whosoever will be great among you, let him be your minister" (Matthew 20:26, KJV).

## Cautions
- Avoid titles that exalt leaders (Matthew 23:8–12).
- Beware of leaders who claim unaccountable authority or demand money for prophecy.
- Prophecies about marriage partners, business deals or political outcomes require careful testing.
- Teach the congregation to weigh every word.`,
    views: `Cessationists (many Reformed and Baptist groups) hold that apostles and prophets ceased. Pentecostals and charismatics widely recognise apostles and prophets today; the New Apostolic Reformation movement uses the titles for ministers with authority over networks, and has been criticised by others. Catholic and Orthodox traditions see apostolic succession in bishops.`,
    pastoral: `Celebrate those called to pioneer, plant, send and strengthen churches. Insist on character, accountability and submission to Scripture. Encourage every member to listen to God and speak His encouragement, subject to the community's discernment.` },
];
